1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,757
It's November eleventh,

2
00:00:01,757 --> 00:00:03,285
nineteen fifty-five.

3
00:00:03,465 --> 00:00:06,204
A cold, bright morning in a small

4
00:00:06,204 --> 00:00:07,366
American town.

5
00:00:07,546 --> 00:00:09,901
The high school band is warming up

6
00:00:09,901 --> 00:00:11,702
at the end of Main Street.

7
00:00:11,882 --> 00:00:14,553
Flags hang from every lamppost.

8
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:16,485
And along the sidewalk,

9
00:00:16,485 --> 00:00:18,085
families are waiting,

10
00:00:18,085 --> 00:00:19,381
bundled in coats,

11
00:00:19,381 --> 00:00:21,362
to watch the men march by.

12
00:00:21,542 --> 00:00:23,016
Men in their thirties,

13
00:00:23,016 --> 00:00:24,423
a little heavier now,

14
00:00:24,423 --> 00:00:27,171
wearing old uniforms that don't quite fit

15
00:00:27,171 --> 00:00:27,707
anymore.

16
00:00:27,887 --> 00:00:28,943
The veterans.

17
00:00:29,294 --> 00:00:30,681
Just a year earlier,

18
00:00:30,681 --> 00:00:32,277
in nineteen fifty-four,

19
00:00:32,277 --> 00:00:34,776
Congress had changed the name of the

20
00:00:34,776 --> 00:00:35,331
holiday.

21
00:00:35,511 --> 00:00:38,814
For decades, November eleventh had been Armistice

22
00:00:38,814 --> 00:00:41,106
Day, honoring the end of the First

23
00:00:41,106 --> 00:00:41,780
World War.

24
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:43,605
Now it was Veterans Day,

25
00:00:43,605 --> 00:00:45,730
to honor all American veterans,

26
00:00:45,730 --> 00:00:48,678
including the more than sixteen million who

27
00:00:48,678 --> 00:00:51,077
had served in the Second World War,

28
00:00:51,077 --> 00:00:53,408
and the millions more who had just

29
00:00:53,408 --> 00:00:54,847
come home from Korea.

30
00:00:55,198 --> 00:00:57,496
So today, let's remember them.

31
00:00:57,676 --> 00:00:59,089
The men, and women,

32
00:00:59,089 --> 00:01:01,469
who came home from war and built

33
00:01:01,469 --> 00:01:03,699
the America many of us grew up

34
00:01:03,699 --> 00:01:03,922
in.

35
00:01:04,102 --> 00:01:05,608
Where they had been,

36
00:01:05,608 --> 00:01:07,340
what they came home to,

37
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:09,072
and what they did next.

38
00:01:09,252 --> 00:01:11,686
If your father or grandfather was one

39
00:01:11,686 --> 00:01:13,594
of them, this one is for him.

40
00:01:13,943 --> 00:01:15,656
In nineteen fifty-five,

41
00:01:15,656 --> 00:01:17,890
most of the men in that parade

42
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:20,198
had been in uniform just ten or

43
00:01:20,198 --> 00:01:21,687
twelve years before.

44
00:01:21,867 --> 00:01:23,726
They had been eighteen,

45
00:01:23,726 --> 00:01:25,908
nineteen, twenty years old.

46
00:01:26,088 --> 00:01:28,310
Farm boys who had never been more

47
00:01:28,310 --> 00:01:30,128
than fifty miles from home.

48
00:01:30,308 --> 00:01:32,653
City kids from the factories.

49
00:01:32,833 --> 00:01:35,483
Young men from every state and every

50
00:01:35,483 --> 00:01:36,293
background.

51
00:01:36,642 --> 00:01:39,516
They had trained in places they'd never

52
00:01:39,516 --> 00:01:42,094
heard of, then shipped out to North

53
00:01:42,094 --> 00:01:43,715
Africa, Italy, France,

54
00:01:43,715 --> 00:01:46,662
Germany, and islands across the Pacific.

55
00:01:46,842 --> 00:01:49,520
They had flown bombers over Europe,

56
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:51,968
served on ships in the Atlantic,

57
00:01:51,968 --> 00:01:54,570
and landed on beaches in Normandy,

58
00:01:54,570 --> 00:01:56,176
Iwo Jima and Okinawa.

59
00:01:56,356 --> 00:01:58,809
More than four hundred thousand Americans did

60
00:01:58,809 --> 00:01:59,572
not come home.

61
00:01:59,922 --> 00:02:01,339
And they were not only men.

62
00:02:01,519 --> 00:02:05,242
About three hundred and fifty thousand American

63
00:02:05,242 --> 00:02:08,490
women served in uniform during the Second

64
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:10,154
World War, as nurses,

65
00:02:10,154 --> 00:02:11,976
pilots ferrying planes,

66
00:02:11,976 --> 00:02:14,986
clerks, mechanics and radio operators.

67
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:18,066
Millions more worked in the factories at

68
00:02:18,066 --> 00:02:18,429
home.

69
00:02:18,609 --> 00:02:20,132
By nineteen fifty-five,

70
00:02:20,132 --> 00:02:22,317
many of those women were mothers,

71
00:02:22,317 --> 00:02:24,634
and some marched in the parade too.

72
00:02:24,984 --> 00:02:27,875
Back home, everyone had done their part.

73
00:02:28,055 --> 00:02:30,859
Families planted victory gardens,

74
00:02:30,859 --> 00:02:32,303
bought war bonds,

75
00:02:32,303 --> 00:02:35,871
saved scrap metal and lived with rationing

76
00:02:35,871 --> 00:02:37,146
of sugar, meat,

77
00:02:37,146 --> 00:02:38,760
gasoline and tires.

78
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:41,265
Mothers hung a little flag with a

79
00:02:41,265 --> 00:02:43,590
blue star in the front window for

80
00:02:43,590 --> 00:02:45,070
every son in uniform.

81
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:48,652
Children collected tin cans and newspapers.

82
00:02:48,832 --> 00:02:51,014
The whole country was in it together.

83
00:02:51,364 --> 00:02:54,608
When the war ended in nineteen forty-five,

84
00:02:54,608 --> 00:02:57,851
millions of servicemen came home almost at

85
00:02:57,851 --> 00:02:58,238
once.

86
00:02:58,418 --> 00:03:01,238
Troop ships sailed into New York,

87
00:03:01,238 --> 00:03:03,375
Boston and San Francisco.

88
00:03:03,555 --> 00:03:05,935
Trains carried men across the country.

89
00:03:06,115 --> 00:03:08,522
Families waited at the station,

90
00:03:08,522 --> 00:03:09,610
holding signs.

91
00:03:09,790 --> 00:03:12,506
Wives and mothers who hadn't seen their

92
00:03:12,506 --> 00:03:14,596
men in three or four years ran

93
00:03:14,596 --> 00:03:15,850
down the platform.

94
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,267
Many of those men had changed.

95
00:03:18,447 --> 00:03:20,675
They had seen things they would never

96
00:03:20,675 --> 00:03:21,338
talk about.

97
00:03:21,518 --> 00:03:23,569
Some came home with wounds,

98
00:03:23,569 --> 00:03:25,163
some with nightmares.

99
00:03:25,343 --> 00:03:26,343
In those days,

100
00:03:26,343 --> 00:03:28,627
people called it battle fatigue,

101
00:03:28,627 --> 00:03:31,197
and most men were simply expected to

102
00:03:31,197 --> 00:03:33,267
put it behind them and get on

103
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:33,981
with life.

104
00:03:34,161 --> 00:03:35,375
Most of them did,

105
00:03:35,375 --> 00:03:38,016
quietly, for the rest of their lives.

106
00:03:38,365 --> 00:03:41,593
Many had married quickly before shipping out,

107
00:03:41,593 --> 00:03:43,816
and now came home to wives they

108
00:03:43,816 --> 00:03:46,971
barely knew, and sometimes children they had

109
00:03:46,971 --> 00:03:47,688
never met.

110
00:03:47,868 --> 00:03:50,761
A three-year-old might hide behind his mother's

111
00:03:50,761 --> 00:03:53,161
skirt when a stranger in uniform walked

112
00:03:53,161 --> 00:03:54,207
through the door.

113
00:03:54,387 --> 00:03:56,530
It took time for those families to

114
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:57,917
become families again,

115
00:03:57,917 --> 00:03:59,240
and most of them did.

116
00:03:59,590 --> 00:04:01,228
And they came home in a hurry

117
00:04:01,228 --> 00:04:02,075
to get started.

118
00:04:02,255 --> 00:04:03,706
They had lost years.

119
00:04:03,886 --> 00:04:05,205
They wanted jobs,

120
00:04:05,205 --> 00:04:07,299
wives, houses and children,

121
00:04:07,299 --> 00:04:09,238
and they wanted them now.

122
00:04:09,418 --> 00:04:10,959
In nineteen forty-six,

123
00:04:10,959 --> 00:04:13,830
a record number of Americans got married.

124
00:04:14,010 --> 00:04:16,544
And then, starting that same year,

125
00:04:16,544 --> 00:04:17,737
came the babies.

126
00:04:17,917 --> 00:04:19,891
The baby boom had begun.

127
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:22,354
The country had made them a promise.

128
00:04:22,534 --> 00:04:24,934
In June of nineteen forty-four,

129
00:04:24,934 --> 00:04:26,947
even before the war ended,

130
00:04:26,947 --> 00:04:31,671
President Roosevelt signed the Servicemen's Readjustment Act.

131
00:04:31,851 --> 00:04:33,360
Everyone called it the G.

132
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:33,819
I.

133
00:04:33,999 --> 00:04:34,300
Bill.

134
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:37,128
It offered veterans money for college or

135
00:04:37,128 --> 00:04:39,644
job training, low-interest home loans,

136
00:04:39,644 --> 00:04:42,027
and help while they looked for work.

137
00:04:42,377 --> 00:04:44,246
The results were astonishing.

138
00:04:44,426 --> 00:04:45,975
By nineteen fifty-six,

139
00:04:45,975 --> 00:04:48,863
about seven point eight million World War

140
00:04:48,863 --> 00:04:50,834
Two veterans had used the G.

141
00:04:51,014 --> 00:04:51,258
I.

142
00:04:51,438 --> 00:04:53,934
Bill for education or training.

143
00:04:54,114 --> 00:04:56,669
More than two million went to college.

144
00:04:56,849 --> 00:04:58,556
In nineteen forty-seven,

145
00:04:58,556 --> 00:05:01,045
veterans made up nearly half of all

146
00:05:01,045 --> 00:05:03,037
college students in America.

147
00:05:03,217 --> 00:05:06,303
Campuses were so crowded that students lived

148
00:05:06,303 --> 00:05:08,337
in Quonset huts and trailers.

149
00:05:08,687 --> 00:05:10,649
Many of those men were the first

150
00:05:10,649 --> 00:05:12,550
in their families ever to go to

151
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:13,040
college.

152
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:16,367
Sons of coal miners became engineers.

153
00:05:16,547 --> 00:05:19,008
Sons of farmers became doctors.

154
00:05:19,188 --> 00:05:21,890
Sons of immigrants became lawyers,

155
00:05:21,890 --> 00:05:23,797
teachers and scientists.

156
00:05:23,977 --> 00:05:24,337
The G.

157
00:05:24,517 --> 00:05:24,807
I.

158
00:05:24,987 --> 00:05:27,910
Bill created a huge new American middle

159
00:05:27,910 --> 00:05:30,084
class in a single generation.

160
00:05:30,434 --> 00:05:33,013
Not every veteran shared equally in those

161
00:05:33,013 --> 00:05:33,580
benefits.

162
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:36,662
Black veterans, especially in the South,

163
00:05:36,662 --> 00:05:39,347
often found that banks would not give

164
00:05:39,347 --> 00:05:39,855
them G.

165
00:05:40,035 --> 00:05:40,395
I.

166
00:05:40,575 --> 00:05:41,730
Bill home loans,

167
00:05:41,730 --> 00:05:44,475
and that many colleges would not admit

168
00:05:44,475 --> 00:05:44,836
them.

169
00:05:45,016 --> 00:05:47,405
Many of those men came home determined

170
00:05:47,405 --> 00:05:48,348
to change that.

171
00:05:48,528 --> 00:05:50,918
Their service, and their frustration,

172
00:05:50,918 --> 00:05:53,502
helped fuel the civil rights movement of

173
00:05:53,502 --> 00:05:55,053
the fifties and sixties.

174
00:05:55,402 --> 00:05:55,832
The G.

175
00:05:56,012 --> 00:05:56,430
I.

176
00:05:56,610 --> 00:05:59,825
Bill also backed millions of home loans,

177
00:05:59,825 --> 00:06:01,835
often with no money down.

178
00:06:02,015 --> 00:06:04,096
That's how many of the new suburbs

179
00:06:04,096 --> 00:06:04,708
got built.

180
00:06:04,888 --> 00:06:07,516
Places like Levittown were full of veterans

181
00:06:07,516 --> 00:06:09,044
and their young families.

182
00:06:09,224 --> 00:06:10,810
By nineteen fifty-five,

183
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:12,879
a man who had been sleeping in

184
00:06:12,879 --> 00:06:15,361
a foxhole ten years earlier might be

185
00:06:15,361 --> 00:06:17,636
mowing his own lawn on a Saturday

186
00:06:17,636 --> 00:06:18,187
morning.

187
00:06:18,538 --> 00:06:20,165
And then they went to work,

188
00:06:20,165 --> 00:06:21,068
and they built.

189
00:06:21,248 --> 00:06:22,541
In nineteen fifty-five,

190
00:06:22,541 --> 00:06:24,563
the veterans of the Second World War

191
00:06:24,563 --> 00:06:25,856
were in their thirties,

192
00:06:25,856 --> 00:06:27,541
at the peak of their strength.

193
00:06:27,721 --> 00:06:30,142
They worked in the steel mills,

194
00:06:30,142 --> 00:06:32,876
the auto plants and the oil fields.

195
00:06:33,056 --> 00:06:34,602
They built highways,

196
00:06:34,602 --> 00:06:36,380
bridges, dams, schools,

197
00:06:36,380 --> 00:06:38,698
hospitals and whole new towns.

198
00:06:39,049 --> 00:06:41,295
The skills they'd learned in the war

199
00:06:41,295 --> 00:06:42,543
came home with them.

200
00:06:42,723 --> 00:06:45,835
Men who had repaired airplanes became mechanics.

201
00:06:46,015 --> 00:06:49,985
Radio operators became electricians and engineers.

202
00:06:50,165 --> 00:06:52,336
Navy cooks opened diners.

203
00:06:52,516 --> 00:06:54,827
Army engineers built roads.

204
00:06:55,007 --> 00:06:57,363
Pilots flew for the new airlines.

205
00:06:57,543 --> 00:07:00,179
Medics became doctors and nurses.

206
00:07:00,359 --> 00:07:02,773
The war had trained a generation of

207
00:07:02,773 --> 00:07:03,877
skilled workers.

208
00:07:04,227 --> 00:07:06,177
They also started businesses.

209
00:07:06,357 --> 00:07:08,802
Gas stations, hardware stores,

210
00:07:08,802 --> 00:07:12,061
car dealerships, construction companies,

211
00:07:12,061 --> 00:07:14,914
insurance agencies and restaurants.

212
00:07:15,094 --> 00:07:18,315
Many small businesses on American Main Streets

213
00:07:18,315 --> 00:07:21,116
in the fifties were started by veterans,

214
00:07:21,116 --> 00:07:23,427
often with a small loan backed by

215
00:07:23,427 --> 00:07:23,847
the G.

216
00:07:24,027 --> 00:07:24,434
I.

217
00:07:24,614 --> 00:07:24,985
Bill.

218
00:07:25,165 --> 00:07:27,555
Some of them grew into companies that

219
00:07:27,555 --> 00:07:28,718
still exist today.

220
00:07:29,068 --> 00:07:31,108
And they brought the war's habits home

221
00:07:31,108 --> 00:07:31,645
with them.

222
00:07:31,825 --> 00:07:33,880
They were organized and disciplined.

223
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:35,228
They got up early,

224
00:07:35,228 --> 00:07:36,655
made their beds tight,

225
00:07:36,655 --> 00:07:38,147
and shined their shoes.

226
00:07:38,327 --> 00:07:40,260
They knew how to work in teams

227
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:41,292
and take orders,

228
00:07:41,292 --> 00:07:42,646
and how to give them.

229
00:07:42,826 --> 00:07:45,459
Many companies of the fifties were run

230
00:07:45,459 --> 00:07:46,706
like the military,

231
00:07:46,706 --> 00:07:48,785
by men who had learned to lead

232
00:07:48,785 --> 00:07:49,548
in uniform.

233
00:07:49,898 --> 00:07:50,826
And they joined.

234
00:07:51,006 --> 00:07:53,559
The American Legion and the Veterans of

235
00:07:53,559 --> 00:07:56,242
Foreign Wars grew to millions of members.

236
00:07:56,422 --> 00:07:58,552
Veterans ran for city council,

237
00:07:58,552 --> 00:08:01,533
served on school boards and coached Little

238
00:08:01,533 --> 00:08:02,030
League.

239
00:08:02,210 --> 00:08:04,277
Many went into politics.

240
00:08:04,457 --> 00:08:07,159
For decades, a majority of the members

241
00:08:07,159 --> 00:08:09,008
of Congress were veterans.

242
00:08:09,188 --> 00:08:11,544
Seven men who served in the Second

243
00:08:11,544 --> 00:08:13,832
World War later became president.

244
00:08:14,182 --> 00:08:15,801
By nineteen fifty-five,

245
00:08:15,801 --> 00:08:18,265
there was a newer group of veterans

246
00:08:18,265 --> 00:08:18,547
too.

247
00:08:18,727 --> 00:08:20,953
The Korean War had begun in June

248
00:08:20,953 --> 00:08:23,317
of nineteen fifty and ended with a

249
00:08:23,317 --> 00:08:26,239
ceasefire in July of nineteen fifty-three.

250
00:08:26,419 --> 00:08:29,618
About one point eight million Americans served

251
00:08:29,618 --> 00:08:30,731
in Korea itself,

252
00:08:30,731 --> 00:08:33,861
and more than thirty-six thousand died there.

253
00:08:34,211 --> 00:08:37,009
Korea was often called the forgotten war.

254
00:08:37,189 --> 00:08:39,288
The men who came home in fifty-three

255
00:08:39,288 --> 00:08:41,504
didn't get the big parades their older

256
00:08:41,504 --> 00:08:42,262
brothers had.

257
00:08:42,442 --> 00:08:44,427
The country was tired of war,

258
00:08:44,427 --> 00:08:47,028
and many people barely paid attention.

259
00:08:47,208 --> 00:08:49,200
But in towns across America,

260
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,975
on Veterans Day of nineteen fifty-five,

261
00:08:51,975 --> 00:08:55,177
those younger men marched alongside the older

262
00:08:55,177 --> 00:08:55,532
ones.

263
00:08:55,883 --> 00:08:58,414
Some men had served in both wars.

264
00:08:58,594 --> 00:09:00,938
They had been called back from the

265
00:09:00,938 --> 00:09:02,800
reserves in nineteen fifty,

266
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,282
just as they were settling down with

267
00:09:05,282 --> 00:09:06,523
families and jobs.

268
00:09:06,703 --> 00:09:09,211
For them, Korea meant leaving home a

269
00:09:09,211 --> 00:09:10,047
second time.

270
00:09:10,227 --> 00:09:11,545
They came back again,

271
00:09:11,545 --> 00:09:13,303
and went right back to work.

272
00:09:13,654 --> 00:09:15,306
By nineteen fifty-five,

273
00:09:15,306 --> 00:09:18,036
the military was also part of everyday

274
00:09:18,036 --> 00:09:19,400
life for young men.

275
00:09:19,580 --> 00:09:21,547
The draft was still in place,

276
00:09:21,547 --> 00:09:23,921
and most young men served two years

277
00:09:23,921 --> 00:09:25,142
after high school.

278
00:09:25,322 --> 00:09:28,119
Many were stationed in Germany,

279
00:09:28,119 --> 00:09:29,472
Japan or Korea,

280
00:09:29,472 --> 00:09:32,810
or on bases across the United States.

281
00:09:32,990 --> 00:09:36,068
Almost every family on the street had

282
00:09:36,068 --> 00:09:38,480
a son, a brother or a husband

283
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:40,560
in uniform at some point.

284
00:09:40,910 --> 00:09:44,054
So picture that parade on November eleventh,

285
00:09:44,054 --> 00:09:45,484
nineteen fifty-five.

286
00:09:45,664 --> 00:09:46,507
At the front,

287
00:09:46,507 --> 00:09:48,648
a color guard carrying the flags.

288
00:09:48,828 --> 00:09:50,551
Then the high school band,

289
00:09:50,551 --> 00:09:52,009
playing a Sousa march.

290
00:09:52,189 --> 00:09:54,380
Then a few elderly men from the

291
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:55,511
First World War,

292
00:09:55,511 --> 00:09:56,995
now in their sixties,

293
00:09:56,995 --> 00:09:58,621
riding in convertibles.

294
00:09:58,801 --> 00:10:01,105
And then the men from the Second

295
00:10:01,105 --> 00:10:02,546
World War and Korea,

296
00:10:02,546 --> 00:10:04,202
marching in loose rows,

297
00:10:04,202 --> 00:10:06,579
trying to remember how to stay in

298
00:10:06,579 --> 00:10:06,939
step.

299
00:10:07,290 --> 00:10:09,484
Their wives watched from the sidewalk.

300
00:10:09,664 --> 00:10:12,112
Their children sat on their shoulders or

301
00:10:12,112 --> 00:10:13,275
waved little flags.

302
00:10:13,455 --> 00:10:15,731
Some of the kids didn't really understand

303
00:10:15,731 --> 00:10:17,286
what their fathers had done.

304
00:10:17,466 --> 00:10:19,891
They just knew Dad looked different in

305
00:10:19,891 --> 00:10:22,443
a uniform, and that the grown-ups around

306
00:10:22,443 --> 00:10:25,059
them were standing very straight and very

307
00:10:25,059 --> 00:10:25,442
quiet.

308
00:10:25,792 --> 00:10:27,132
At eleven o'clock,

309
00:10:27,132 --> 00:10:29,961
the eleventh hour of the eleventh day,

310
00:10:29,961 --> 00:10:31,376
the parade stopped.

311
00:10:31,556 --> 00:10:33,297
A minister said a prayer.

312
00:10:33,477 --> 00:10:35,242
A bugler played Taps.

313
00:10:35,422 --> 00:10:37,246
The names of the men from town

314
00:10:37,246 --> 00:10:39,010
who had died were read aloud.

315
00:10:39,190 --> 00:10:41,419
Mothers who had lost sons stood a

316
00:10:41,419 --> 00:10:43,918
little apart, and other women went to

317
00:10:43,918 --> 00:10:45,134
stand beside them.

318
00:10:45,484 --> 00:10:47,144
Then the parade broke up.

319
00:10:47,324 --> 00:10:49,681
The men went to the American Legion

320
00:10:49,681 --> 00:10:50,758
hall for coffee,

321
00:10:50,758 --> 00:10:53,384
doughnuts and maybe something stronger.

322
00:10:53,564 --> 00:10:55,100
They told a few stories,

323
00:10:55,100 --> 00:10:56,571
but only to each other.

324
00:10:56,751 --> 00:10:58,242
Then they went home,

325
00:10:58,242 --> 00:11:00,105
had lunch, and on Monday,

326
00:11:00,105 --> 00:11:02,416
went back to work at the plant,

327
00:11:02,416 --> 00:11:04,205
the store or the office.

328
00:11:04,555 --> 00:11:07,098
Many towns also had a war memorial

329
00:11:07,098 --> 00:11:08,519
in the town square,

330
00:11:08,519 --> 00:11:11,138
with the names of their dead carved

331
00:11:11,138 --> 00:11:13,158
in stone or cast in bronze.

332
00:11:13,338 --> 00:11:15,159
After the Second World War,

333
00:11:15,159 --> 00:11:17,723
new names were added beneath the names

334
00:11:17,723 --> 00:11:19,410
from the First World War.

335
00:11:19,590 --> 00:11:20,814
On Veterans Day,

336
00:11:20,814 --> 00:11:22,957
families laid wreaths there.

337
00:11:23,137 --> 00:11:26,196
Children traced the letters with their fingers,

338
00:11:26,196 --> 00:11:28,930
looking for their uncles and grandfathers.

339
00:11:29,280 --> 00:11:32,085
Historians and writers have called them the

340
00:11:32,085 --> 00:11:33,390
Greatest Generation.

341
00:11:33,570 --> 00:11:35,503
Most of them would have laughed at

342
00:11:35,503 --> 00:11:35,787
that.

343
00:11:35,967 --> 00:11:38,707
They didn't think of themselves as heroes.

344
00:11:38,887 --> 00:11:40,940
They thought they had just done their

345
00:11:40,940 --> 00:11:42,660
job, the same as everyone else.

346
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:44,026
But what they did,

347
00:11:44,026 --> 00:11:45,937
both in the war and after it,

348
00:11:45,937 --> 00:11:47,914
shaped the country we live in.

349
00:11:48,264 --> 00:11:50,342
They raised the baby boomers.

350
00:11:50,522 --> 00:11:51,929
They built the suburbs,

351
00:11:51,929 --> 00:11:53,703
the highways and the schools.

352
00:11:53,883 --> 00:11:56,193
They made the American middle class.

353
00:11:56,373 --> 00:11:58,746
They sent their children to college in

354
00:11:58,746 --> 00:12:00,994
numbers no generation had ever seen.

355
00:12:01,174 --> 00:12:03,293
And they rarely asked for anything in

356
00:12:03,293 --> 00:12:03,693
return.

357
00:12:04,044 --> 00:12:06,667
Today, almost all of them are gone.

358
00:12:06,847 --> 00:12:10,062
Of the sixteen million Americans who served

359
00:12:10,062 --> 00:12:11,856
in the Second World War,

360
00:12:11,856 --> 00:12:14,398
only a very small number are still

361
00:12:14,398 --> 00:12:16,940
alive, nearly all of them close to

362
00:12:16,940 --> 00:12:18,435
a hundred years old,

363
00:12:18,435 --> 00:12:19,108
or older.

364
00:12:19,288 --> 00:12:21,877
Many of their stories went untold.

365
00:12:22,057 --> 00:12:23,972
Their medals sit in drawers.

366
00:12:24,152 --> 00:12:27,020
Their letters are in shoeboxes in attics.

367
00:12:27,370 --> 00:12:29,991
If your father or grandfather served,

368
00:12:29,991 --> 00:12:31,549
go find those letters.

369
00:12:31,729 --> 00:12:33,320
Look at the photographs.

370
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:35,658
Ask the older members of your family

371
00:12:35,658 --> 00:12:36,797
what they remember.

372
00:12:36,977 --> 00:12:37,929
Write it down.

373
00:12:38,109 --> 00:12:40,882
Their stories are the story of America,

374
00:12:40,882 --> 00:12:43,299
and they deserve to be remembered.

375
00:12:43,649 --> 00:12:45,587
So tell us in the comments.

376
00:12:45,767 --> 00:12:47,394
Who in your family served,

377
00:12:47,394 --> 00:12:48,020
and where?

378
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:50,416
What do you remember about them,

379
00:12:50,416 --> 00:12:52,287
and what did they tell you,

380
00:12:52,287 --> 00:12:53,395
or not tell you,

381
00:12:53,395 --> 00:12:54,365
about the war?

382
00:12:54,545 --> 00:12:55,967
We read every comment,

383
00:12:55,967 --> 00:12:57,972
and we would be honored to hear

384
00:12:57,972 --> 00:12:58,747
their names.

385
00:12:58,927 --> 00:13:00,656
Thank you for spending this time with

386
00:13:00,656 --> 00:13:00,797
me.

387
00:13:00,977 --> 00:13:02,997
And to every veteran watching,

388
00:13:02,997 --> 00:13:03,670
thank you.
